---
import Adr from "../../components/Adr.astro";
import Excerpt from "../../components/Excerpt.astro";
import Layer from "../../components/Layer.astro";
import Layout from "../../components/Layout.astro";
import PageLink from "../../components/PageLink.astro";
import Path from "../../components/Path.astro";
import Rule from "../../components/Rule.astro";

const VALUES = "server/src/iki_server/entities/values.py";
const ACCESS = "server/src/iki_server/entities/access.py";
const INCLUDED = "server/src/iki_server/runs/included.py";
const QUEUE = "server/src/iki_server/runs/queue.py";

const H = {
  short: "A reference shows only what its reader can read",
  how: "Where references are masked",
  example: "A request that names a sample the service can't read",
  rules: "Readers, runs and leases",
  ref: "Code and decisions",
};
const toc = [
  { id: "short", label: H.short },
  { id: "how", label: H.how },
  { id: "example", label: H.example },
  { id: "rules", label: H.rules },
  { id: "ref", label: H.ref },
];

const service = `{
  "private_sample": "unreadable",
  "comparison_samples": ["<the shared sample's ID>", "unreadable"],
  "sample_notes": [{ "sample": "unreadable", "note": "Comparison" }]
}`;
---

<Layout toc={toc}>
  <Layer n={1} title={H.short}>
    <p>
      A reader can read a record but not everything it refers to. Wherever Iki shows a record's
      values, a reference to a record the reader can't read is the string <code>unreadable</code>{" "}
      instead of an ID: no ID, type or title, in single references, lists and rows alike (<Adr
        n={[66, 132]}
      />). A run sees its references as its project could at launch: positions it couldn't read are
      pinned as <code>unreadable</code>, and the worker receives them so. Each lease checks again that
      the run's project can still read everything else the command will receive, and the run ends if
      it can't (<Adr n={[66, 105]} />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={2} title={H.how}>
    <p>
      Serializing a value for a reader takes the IDs that reader may identify. Anything else becomes
      the marker:
    </p>
    <Excerpt file={VALUES} start="^def to_json\(" />
    <p>
      The IDs come from <code>readable_reference_ids</code>: records linked into a project the
      reader reads, registry entries they hold a record of, and tombstones of purged records they
      could read (<Path p={ACCESS} sym="def readable_reference_ids(" />). Values already stored as
      JSON, such as a revision's, go through the same boundary (<Path
        p={VALUES}
        sym="def redact_json_references("
      />).
    </p>
    <p>
      That covers record and revision values over REST and MCP, search, lists, revisions and their
      diffs, registry entries' values and history, an amount movement's counterpart, and a project
      webhook's counterpart (<Path p="server/src/iki_server/api/amounts.py" sym="def movement_out(" />,{" "}
      <Path p="server/src/iki_server/webhooks/events.py" sym="def project_amount_movement(" />). CSV
      exports write "An entity you can’t read" in its place. The web app shows the same words, with no
      link (<Path p="web/app/src/pages/entity.tsx" />, <Path p="web/app/src/entity-values.ts" />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={3} title={H.example}>
    <p>
      A project asks a core for the purity of its kinases and sends one of its samples. The request also names a
      second sample it doesn't send: a private one, in a single reference, a list beside the shared
      sample, and a row's cell. The core's technician reads the request, and its values come back as:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{service}</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <code>refs</code> map holds only the shared sample, and the private sample's ID and HRID
      appear nowhere in the answer. The asking project's own Editor reads the same request with the
      private sample's ID. A run the core launches on the request gets the same values in its worker
      document (<Path
        p="server/tests/test_requests.py"
        sym="def test_a_request_indicates_a_reference_the_service_cannot_read("
      />, <Path
        p="server/tests/test_run_inclusions.py"
        sym="def test_a_service_commands_reference_values_match_its_project("
      />).
    </p>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={4} title={H.rules}>
    <div class="rules">
      <Rule title="A run pins its project's view at launch" open>
        <p>
          At launch, Iki walks every typed reference in the run's inputs and included records, at
          their pinned revisions, and records the positions its project can't identify as the run's{" "}
          <code>reference_mask</code>. Retries, reuse and exact reproduction keep that view, including
          after a bundle import, and the mask counts in the reuse key (<Adr n="0105" />, <Path
            p={INCLUDED}
            sym="def reference_mask("
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/reuse.py" sym="reference_mask" />). What else a run
          pins, and its provenance, is on <PageLink to="Pinning and provenance" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Each lease checks current access">
        <p>
          Before a worker gets a job, the lease checks that the launcher can still run on that tier in
          the project, and builds the command's input document from the pins. That needs every pinned
          record, every reference it doesn't mask, and every file param to be readable in the run's
          project now. If one isn't, the attempt ends with a generic reason; a packed command checks
          each of its runs on its own, and leaves out those that fail (<Adr n="0066" />, <Path p={QUEUE} sym="def lease(" />, <Path
            p={INCLUDED}
            sym="def manifest("
          />):
        </p>
        <Excerpt file={INCLUDED} start="^class Unavailable\(" />
        <p>
          Results the run already made stay in its project, like a copy someone made while they could
          read the input (<Path
            p="server/tests/test_run_inclusions.py"
            sym="def test_a_lease_requires_the_pinned_records_in_the_run_project("
          />). How leases work is on <PageLink to="The worker and its leases" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Launching and reproducing need the person's access">
        <p>
          A launcher must be able to read each reference an input includes; one they can't is refused
          with the input it came from (<Path p={INCLUDED} sym="class Unreadable(" />). A reproduction
          reads what the run read, at those revisions, and is refused if the person can't read them
          now (<Adr n="0105" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Pages about runs keep each input's place">
        <p>
          Run and workflow pages show inputs and inclusions as each reader can read them now. An
          unreadable one keeps its place in the list, as "An entity you can’t read". A comparison of a
          reproduction shows record values only to readers who can read what they refer to (<Adr
            n="0066"
          />, <Path p="server/src/iki_server/runs/output_refs.py" sym="def comparisons(" />).
        </p>
      </Rule>
      <Rule title="Exact copies need every reference">
        <p>
          A signed export or a bundle carries exact history and signed bytes, so it's made only for a
          reader who can read every reference in it (<Path p={ACCESS} sym="def all_references_readable(" />).
          Exports are on <PageLink to="Exports" />.
        </p>
      </Rule>
    </div>
  </Layer>

  <Layer n={5} title={H.ref}>
    <div class="refs">
      <h3>Where</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Path p={VALUES} sym='UNREADABLE_REFERENCE = "unreadable"' />: the marker</li>
        <li><Path p={ACCESS} />: which references a reader or a project can identify</li>
        <li><Path p={INCLUDED} />: a run's pinned references, its mask and its manifest</li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Decisions</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><Adr n="0066" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0105" title /></li>
        <li><Adr n="0132" title /></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Layer>
</Layout>
